// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/SPEC.md sections 2.6 and 2.7 (literal forms).
//
// The lexer reads decimal digits and stops. So:
//   0x1F   lexes as the integer 0 followed by the identifier x1F
//   1_000  lexes as the integer 1 followed by the identifier _000
//   1e9    lexes as the integer 1 followed by the identifier e9
//
// These are now REJECTED, and it is worth being precise about why, because it
// is not the rule this file was written to test. The checker reports
//   error: unknown identifier 'x1F'
// It caught the second token, not the malformed literal. The lexer still splits
// `0x1F` into two tokens and still assigns `a` the value 0; nothing yet knows
// that `0x1F` was meant to be one number. Before name resolution landed, every
// line here passed with exit 0 and emitted `int64_t a = 0; x1F;`.
//
// So the diagnostic is accidental cover, not the rule. A conforming
// implementation must either support these literal forms or reject them AS
// literals, with a message that names the real problem.

pub fn f() {
    let copy a = 0x1F
    let copy b = 1_000
    let copy c = 1e9
}
